From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vincent Keyan <vincent.keyan@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 13:46:38 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] patch_nso: keep the split where the back patch area reaches

A module with two patch areas (TWO) split its sites at 128 MB less the
front area and 1 MB. The back area sits after the image, bss and the key
page, and reaches no site more than 128 MB below it. With a long ro, data
and bss after the text (Starlink: Battle for Atlas main: 172.5 MB of
text, 247 MB of image, 16.5 MB of bss, BACK_OFF 0xfc00000) the sites
between 0x6f00000 and 0x7c00000 were out of reach of both areas, and the
first of them stopped the rewrite in B().

SPLIT is now never below what the back area reaches (BACK_REACH), and the
front area starts just small enough to reach up to it; the rerun with
DM_PATCH_FRONT still sizes it exactly and keeps the same split. A module
whose back area already reaches the old split is rewritten as before.
B() now says to, frm and off when a branch is out of reach.

Upstream-Status: pending
Reason: Starlink: Battle for Atlas main could not be rewritten (B() out of reach in the back patch area)
Issue: none yet (Starlink: Battle for Atlas main rewrite)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 dm/tools/nso_asm.py        |  4 +++-
 dm/tools/patch_nso.py      | 16 ++++++++++++----
 docs/reference/rewriter.md |  1 +
 3 files changed, 16 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dm/tools/nso_asm.py b/dm/tools/nso_asm.py
index db941fc..554dccf 100644
--- a/dm/tools/nso_asm.py
+++ b/dm/tools/nso_asm.py
@@ -1,7 +1,9 @@
 # AArch64 instruction encoders of the NSO rewriter (dm/tools/patch_nso.py) and of the per-build patch files (dm/tools/sdk_patches.py, dm/games/<id>/patches.py),
 # which assemble their stubs with them. Each returns the instruction word; a branch asserts its reach
 def B(frm, to):
-    off = to - frm; assert -(1 << 27) <= off < (1 << 27) and off % 4 == 0
+    off = to - frm
+    if not (-(1 << 27) <= off < (1 << 27) and off % 4 == 0):
+        raise AssertionError(f'B out of reach: to={to:#x} frm={frm:#x} off={off:#x}' + ('' if -(1 << 27) <= off < (1 << 27) else ' (beyond ±128 MiB)') + ('' if off % 4 == 0 else ' (misaligned)'))
     return 0x14000000 | ((off >> 2) & 0x3FFFFFF)
 def STP(t, t2, n, off): return 0xA9000000 | (((off // 8) & 0x7F) << 15) | (t2 << 10) | (n << 5) | t
 def LDP(t, t2, n, off): return 0xA9400000 | (((off // 8) & 0x7F) << 15) | (t2 << 10) | (n << 5) | t
diff --git a/dm/tools/patch_nso.py b/dm/tools/patch_nso.py
index d0b76f8..5b0747e 100644
--- a/dm/tools/patch_nso.py
+++ b/dm/tools/patch_nso.py
@@ -46,11 +46,18 @@ BIG_TEXT = FAR and MOD.text_end + FRONT > 0x8000000 - 0x10000
 # A text segment longer than the reach itself (EA SPORTS FC 27: 185.6 MB of text in main) leaves no gap before the base from which a B reaches its end, so the patch area is split in
 # two (TWO): the front area before the base takes the sites below SPLIT, the back area right after the image (after bss, the key page and the frame step page, at BACK_OFF) takes
 # the others. A site's stubs, and their branches back to it, stay in the area it belongs to; SPLIT keeps 1 MB of margin for a stub's exits past its site (a window's targets, a
-# switch's cases). The front area starts at 16 MB; when its sites need more, the rewriter runs again with exactly that size (DM_PATCH_FRONT), and B() checks every branch
+# switch's cases). The front area starts at 16 MB; when its sites need more, the rewriter runs again with exactly that size (DM_PATCH_FRONT), and B() checks every branch.
+# The back area reaches no lower than 128 MB (less the margin) below BACK_OFF, so SPLIT is never below that (BACK_REACH): with a long ro, data and bss after the text (Starlink: Battle for
+# Atlas main, 172.5 MB of text, 247 MB of image and 16.5 MB of bss, BACK_OFF 0xfc00000) the front area then starts small enough to reach up to it, and the rerun sizes it exactly; its
+# stubs follow the sites in address order, so the sites right below SPLIT have theirs at the end of the front area, right before the base
 TWO = BIG_TEXT and MOD.text_end > 0x8000000 - 0x10000
-if TWO: FRONT = int(os.environ.get('DM_PATCH_FRONT', '0'), 0) or 0x1000000
+if TWO:
+    BACK_OFF = (((len(MOD.image) + bss + 0x3FFF) & ~0x3FFF) + 0x8000 + 0xFFFF) & ~0xFFFF   # after the key page and the frame step page FRAME_STEP may take (as KEY_PAGE_OFF below)
+    BACK_REACH = BACK_OFF - 0x8000000 + 0x100000
+    FRONT = int(os.environ.get('DM_PATCH_FRONT', '0'), 0) or min(0x1000000, (0x8000000 - 0x100000 - BACK_REACH) & ~0xFFFF)
+    if FRONT < 0x10000: sys.exit(f'[RED] {sys.argv[3]}: the back patch area at {BACK_OFF:#x} reaches no site below {BACK_REACH:#x}, which leaves the front area no room before the base')
 elif BIG_TEXT: FRONT = int(os.environ.get('DM_PATCH_FRONT', '0'), 0) or (0x8000000 - 0x10000 - MOD.text_end) & ~0xFFFF
-SPLIT = 0x8000000 - FRONT - 0x100000 if TWO else None
+SPLIT = max(0x8000000 - FRONT - 0x100000, BACK_REACH) if TWO else None
 IMG = MOD.image
 ORIG_TEXT = bytes(IMG[:MOD.text_end])   # the text as the file has it, before any cut or site: <name>.changed.tsv compares against it
 if not FAR: IMG.extend(bytes(bss))
@@ -60,7 +67,8 @@ if FAR:
     FILE_IMAGE_LEN = (len(IMG) + 0x3FFF) & ~0x3FFF
     KEY_PAGE_OFF = (len(IMG) + bss + 0x3FFF) & ~0x3FFF
     IMAGE_SIZE, PATCH_OFF = KEY_PAGE_OFF + 0x4000, -FRONT
-    BACK_OFF = (KEY_PAGE_OFF + 0x8000 + 0xFFFF) & ~0xFFFF if TWO else 0   # after the key page and the frame step page FRAME_STEP may take
+    if TWO: assert BACK_OFF == (KEY_PAGE_OFF + 0x8000 + 0xFFFF) & ~0xFFFF, 'BACK_OFF computed before the image layout no longer matches it'
+    else: BACK_OFF = 0
 else:
     KEY_PAGE_OFF = (len(IMG) + 0x3FFF) & ~0x3FFF
     IMG.extend(bytes(0x4000))
diff --git a/docs/reference/rewriter.md b/docs/reference/rewriter.md
index 7bf0c81..7c3d073 100644
--- a/docs/reference/rewriter.md
+++ b/docs/reference/rewriter.md
@@ -283,6 +283,7 @@ To enroll a cut for any game's own module:
 - **Cause:** a text longer than the ±128 MB a `b` reaches has no place for one patch area from which every site is in reach: before the base the last sites are too far, after the image the first ones.
 - **Fix:** such a module (`TWO` in `patch_nso.py`: the text alone ends past the reach) gets two patch areas. The front one, 16 MB before the base, takes the sites below `SPLIT` (128 MB less the front area and 1 MB of margin for a stub's exits past its site); the back one, right after bss, the key page and the frame step page (`BACK_OFF`, 64 KB aligned), takes the others. A site's stubs and their branches back stay in its own area (`use_area`); when the front sites need more room, the rewriter runs again with exactly that size, and `B()` checks every branch. The file is the image, the front area padded to 16 KB, then the back area; `fixups.tsv` adds `BACK_OFF` and `BACK_LEN`, and `IMAGE_SIZE` covers the back area. The Mac loader copies it to `BACK_OFF` and seals it read-only and executable, the iOS embedding gives it a fourth section (`gen_embed.py`: `__TEXT,__dm<name>x2`) that the loader remaps there, and `svcQueryMemory` reports it as CodeData+RX like a patch area after the image (data and bss end at it). A module that does not need it is rewritten byte for byte as before.
 - **Found on:** [EA SPORTS FC 27](../../dm/games/0100C49025D3E000/README.md) `main` (1.8d.310c): 185.6 MB of text, 177,058 sites; 3.9 MB of stubs in front and 3.2 MB at `BACK_OFF` `0xdec0000`.
+- **Then:** the back area reaches no site more than 128 MB below `BACK_OFF`, so with a long ro, data and bss after the text the sites right above `SPLIT` were out of reach of both areas (`B()`: `to=0x6f00578 frm=0xfc02320 off=-0x8d01da8`). `SPLIT` is now never below what the back area reaches (`BACK_REACH`, 128 MB less 1 MB of margin below `BACK_OFF`), and the front area then starts just small enough to reach up to it; the rerun still sizes it exactly. Found on Starlink: Battle for Atlas `main` (v393216): 172.5 MB of text, 247 MB of image, 16.5 MB of bss, `BACK_OFF` `0xfc00000`; 1.7 MB of stubs in front, 0.9 MB in back.
 
 ## Cuts and observation gates
 
